    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 142331\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n431 PM PDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north-northwesterly winds expected Thursday and Friday\n  with gust speeds between 40 and 50 mph.\n\n* Precipitation chances return to the region Tuesday and\n  Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Wednesday.\n\nGusty north winds generally between 20 and 30 mph continue today\nas a closed low weakens into an open wave and lifts eastward out\nof the area. On Thursday, energy left behind from the \naforementioned open wave will combine with a vort lobe riding \nalong the leftover northwesterly flow aloft and will close off \nover southeastern California. This will bring with it a 65-75 kt \njet and corresponding surface north-northeasterly wind gusts \nbetween 40 and 50 mph. This trajectory of winds will result in \neasterly downslope winds, which could bring occasional gusts \nbetween 50 and 60 mph to locations nestled in valleys along the \neastern flanks of area mountains including Interstate 40 east of \nthe Hualapai Mountains in southern Mohave County and Highway 160 \nin Pahrump. As a result, the Wind Advisory in place for the \nColorado River Valley, southern Clark County, and eastern San \nBernardino County from 8 AM Thursday through 11 AM Friday has been\nexpanded to include Death Valley National Park, the Clark County \nMountains, far southern Nye County, and most of Mohave County.\n\nConfidence continues to increase that the closed low over southern \nCalifornia will usher moisture from Hurricane Simon into the \nregion over the weekend. Ensemble means indicate that PWATs will \nincrease to around 0.75 inches across the Mojave Desert and will \nstick around into early next week. That said, without instability,\nthis moisture is unlikely to bring precipitation... However, a \nlongwave trough is progged to swing into central California early \nnext week, which will provide the aforementioned instability \nneeded to bring precipitation chances back to the forecast area. \nRight now, there are 30 to 40 percent chances of precipitation on \nTuesday and Wednesday areawide with snow levels between 8000 and \n9000 feet. Stay tuned as this forecast develops and confidence \nincreases through the week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast\nPackage...Northeasterly to easterly winds around 8-10KT will \nbecome light and variable after sunset, shifting to the northwest \novernight. Mid-morning Thursday, gusty northerly winds return, \nwith gusts to 20-25KT increasing to 25-30KT as winds veer to the \nnortheast by late morning. These winds then persist through the \nend of the forecast period. VFR conditions and clear skies \nprevail.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty winds will\ndiminish this evening, dropping to 5- 10KT overnight across the \nregion. Gusty northerly to northeasterly winds resume areawide by \nThursday morning, with gusts to 25-30KT common thereafter, with \nthe strongest winds gusting to 35-40KT expected along the Lower \nColorado River Valley from KIFP to KEED late morning onward. \nNortheasterly gusts will hold off across the western Mojave \nDesert/KDAG until early afternoon, with gusts there closer to \n20KT. VFR conditions and clear skies prevail.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
